  "worked_solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of $15x+5y=9,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x+5y & =9 \\\\ 5y & =-15x+9 \\\\ y & =-3x+\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of $4y+3px=0,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y+3px & =0 \\\\ 4y & =-3px \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3p}{4}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The slope of the line $15x+5y=9$ is $-3.$\n\n- The slope of the line $4y+3px=0$ is $-\\frac{3p}{4}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-3)\\cdot -\\frac{3p}{4}=-1\\,\u27f9\\,p=-\\frac{4}{9}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
